.TH bc "" "Command"
.SH NAME
bc \- interactive calculator with arbitrary precision
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbc [\-l] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
bc -- interactive calculator with arbitrary precision
Usage:	bc [ -l ] [ file ... ]
	-l	Use the extended bc library
After reading each input 'file', bc reads the standard input.
.HE
.B bc
is a language that performs calculations on numbers with an
arbitrary number of digits.
It is most commonly used as an interactive calculator, where the
user types arithmetic expressions in a syntax reminiscent of C.
.PP
.B bc
recognizes one option:
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Read the extended math library
.B /usr/lib/lib.b
before reading any file or standard input.
.PP
After
.B bc
reads each named
.IR file ,
it reads the standard input.
This lets you load a program stored in a file and then continue
typing at the terminal.
.PP
The delimiters
.B /*
and
.B */
enclose comments.
The three built-in variables
.BR obase ,
.BR ibase ,
and
.B scale
give, respectively, the number base used for printing numbers
(default: ten), the number base used for reading numbers (default:
ten), and the number of digits kept after the decimal point
(default: zero).
Variables may be simple variables or arrays, and need not be
declared, except for variables local to a function.
.PP
.B bc
recognizes the following keywords, with syntax modeled on C:
.IP "\fBif, for, do, while\fR"
Test conditions and define loops.
.IP "\fBbreak, continue\fR"
Alter control flow within a
.B for
or
.B while
loop.
.IP \fBquit\fR
Exit
.B bc
immediately.
.IP "\fBdefine \fIfunction\fB(\fIarg, ..., arg\fB)\fR"
Define a
.B bc
function as a compound statement.
.IP "\fBauto \fIvar, ..., var\fR"
Declare variables local to a function.
.IP "\fBreturn(\fIvalue\fB)\fR"
Return a value from a function.
.PP
.B bc
recognizes the operators
.BR + ", " \- ", " * ", " / ", " % ", " ^ ", " ++ ", " \-\- ", "
.BR = ", " += ", " \-= ", " *= ", " /= ", " %= ", " ^= ", "
.BR == ", " != ", " < ", " <= ", " > ", and " >= ,
similar to C except that
.B ^
and
.B ^=
perform exponentiation.
Statements are separated by semicolons or newlines and may be
grouped into compound statements with braces;
.B bc
prints the value of any statement that is an expression but not an
assignment.
As in the editor
.BR ed ,
a
.B !
at the beginning of a line sends the rest of the line to the shell
as a command.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.5i
	\fB/usr/lib/lib.b\fR	source code for the mathematical library
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B dc
.SH NOTES
Line numbers do not accompany error messages in source files.
.B bc
performs integer calculations with arbitrary precision, limited only
by available memory; results of calculations on numbers with
fractional parts depend on the current
.BR scale .
